(THE DATA, LINES 1 TO 106) xsec
[ (1) (%!PS-Adobe-3.0)
  (7) (/D 64 def /HID 172 def /NL 5 def /NH 8 def /NKV 4 def)
  () (  /V 512 def /SEQ 512 def /HS 8 def /KVD 32 def)
  () (  /KVMUL 2 def /NPARAMS 260032 def) ]
(^The ^model ^card ^is ^one ^line. Line 7 defines width 64, 5~layers, 8~query heads sharing 4 key/value heads and a 512-token vocabulary. Nothing runs yet; names are just defined. Line 1 makes it a print job; lines 2 to 6 are comments for the spooler.)
xrow

/cextra 30 def
/cxproc {
  /dy cyy cld add csz 0.23 mul sub 4 sub def
  1 setgray 0.6 setlinewidth newpath cx 1.6 add dy moveto cx cw add dy lineto stroke
  /cch csz 0.6 mul def
  [ (row 0 bytes) [(96) (91) (7e) (84)] (minus 128) [(22) (17) (-2) (4)] (\327 0.00261005) [(0.0574) (0.0444) (-0.0052) (0.0104)] ] /dt exch def
  0 1 2 { /k exch def /dyy dy 8.4 sub k cld mul sub def
    0.45 setgray /CR csz F cx cgut add dyy moveto dt k 2 mul get show 0 setgray
    0 1 3 { /m exch def cx cgut add 20 m 9 mul add cch mul add dyy moveto dt k 2 mul 1 add get m get rshow } for
  } for } def
[ (16) (/WQ [)
  (17) ([[0.00261005 0.00431596 0.00340043 0.00252913 ... 0.0012534])
  (18) ([<96917e84906c8a8537147183bfa939875d25794a...> <ae99...> ...]]) ]
(^Weights ^are ^bytes. For each matrix row, export.py takes the largest absolute weight m and stores scale~=~m/127. Each weight is rounded to an integer from -127 to 127, plus 128, and written as one byte of a hex string. Two hex characters per weight: that is most of the file.)
xrow

(THE PROGRAM, LINES 107 TO 455) xsec
[ (129) (% o x M mv  ;  M = [scales rows], rows ... offset by 128)
  (130) (/@@mv@@ { /mM exch def /mx exch def /mo exch def)
  (131) (  /mS mM 0 get def /mQ mM 1 get def /mn mx length 1 sub def)
  (132) (  /msx 0 mx { add } forall 128 mul def)
  (133) (  0 1 mQ length 1 sub { /mi exch def /mrow mQ mi get def)
  (134) (    0 0 1 mn { dup mrow exch get exch mx exch get mul add } for)
  (135) (    msx sub mS mi get mul mo mi 3 -1 roll put } for)
  (136) (} bind def) ]
(^mv ^does ^the ^heavy ^lifting. Matrix times vector: about 259,000 multiply-adds per token run through these eight lines. `get on a string returns a byte as an integer. Rather than subtract 128 from every byte, mv takes the raw dot product, subtracts `msx \(128 times the sum of the input\), then applies the row's scale.)
xrow

[ (139) (/rope { /rn exch def /rv exch def)
  (140) (  0 2 rn 1 sub { /ri exch def)
  (141) (    /hd ri HS mod def)
  (142) (    pos 10000 hd HS div exp div @@57.29577951@@ mul /ang exch def)
  (143) (    /fcr ang @@cos@@ def /fci ang @@sin@@ def) ]
(^RoPE, ^in ^degrees. Rotary embeddings turn query/key pairs by `pos~/~10000^\(hd/8\) radians, but PostScript's `sin and `cos take degrees. Hence 57.29577951 \(180/pi\). Without it, every position would get the wrong rotation.)
xrow

[ (118) (SEED @@srand@@ 4 { rand pop } repeat)
  (204) (/r @@rand@@ 2147483647 div ssum mul def /cum 0 def)
  () (  /pick V 1 sub def)
  (205) (0 1 V 1 sub { /j exch def /cum cum logits j get add def)
  () (  cum r gt { /pick j def exit } if } for) ]
(^Sampling ^with ^rand. The logits become a softmax at temperature 0.8. PostScript's built-in `rand picks a point along the total, and the loop walks the running sum until it passes it. Line 118 skips four tiny first draws. Seed 42: same interpreter, same story.)
xrow

[ (257) (% ... typesetting: the same print job lays out the page ...)
  (368) (fs /Times-Roman F ...)
  (449) (9 /Helvetica-Bold F 72 79 moveto \(Host compute: ...\) show)
  (453) (@@showpage@@) ]
(^Then, ^and ^only ^then, ^toner. After 200 tokens \(or an end-of-story token\), the same job word-wraps the story in Times-Roman, draws the run card and calls `showpage. Until then, the printer was just a computer.)
xrow
/rowsend ry def

% ---------- print it yourself ----------
/BB 84 def
/STH 20 def
/PBX BB STH add 8 add def     % print box bottom
/PBH2 96 def /PBY2 PBX PBH2 add def
0 setgray 1 setlinewidth LM PBX CW PBH2 rectp stroke
LM PBY2 14 sub CW 14 rectp fill
1 setgray /HB 7 F LM 10 add PBY2 9.6 sub moveto (PRINT IT YOURSELF) 1 tshow
/H 6.6 F RM 10 sub PBY2 9.6 sub moveto (BRING YOUR OWN PRINTER) 0.8 rtshow 0 setgray
/CB 10.5 F LM 12 add PBY2 33 sub moveto (nc <printer-ip> 9100 < toner1.ps) show
0.4 setgray /H 6.6 F RM 12 sub PBY2 27.5 sub moveto (TONER-1 Pro: toner1_pro.ps, 12.2M parameters, 24.9 MB, ours.) rshow
RM 12 sub PBY2 36 sub moveto (Same command. About 28 times slower.) rshow 0 setgray
LM 12 add RM 12 sub PBY2 44 sub 0.4 hline
[ (^Needs ^real ^PostScript. A PostScript interpreter, Level 2 or later, is required. PCL- or GDI-only printers may print the file as raw text.)
  (^Expect ^minutes. An estimated 0.3 to 5 tok/s is roughly 1 to 10 minutes per 200-token page. It is thinking.)
  (^Mind ^the ^limits. Long jobs can hit the job timeout or the VM limit. Ghostscript uses about 3.5~MB of VM.)
  (^Ask ^first. Only use printers you own or have permission to use. The queue behind you has feelings.) ] /notes exch def
/nw2 CW 24 sub 16 sub 2 div def
0 1 3 { /k exch def
  /nx2 LM 12 add k 2 mod nw2 16 add mul add def
  /ny2 PBY2 55 sub k 2 idiv 22 mul sub def
  newpath nx2 ny2 2.8 add 1.8 0 360 arc fill
  /wfR /TR def /wfB /HB def /wsz 8.2 def /wld 9.9 def /wbs 0.92 def
  notes k get nx2 7 add ny2 nw2 7 sub wrap } for

% ---------- status ----------
gsave 0.925 setgray LM BB CW STH rectp fill grestore
/HB 7 F LM 10 add BB 7.3 add moveto (STATUS) 1 tshow
/box6 { % x y checked
  /bck exch def /byy exch def /bxx exch def
  0.7 setlinewidth bxx byy 6.4 6.4 rectp gsave 1 setgray fill grestore stroke
  bck { 1.1 setlinewidth newpath bxx 1.3 add byy 3.2 add moveto bxx 2.8 add byy 1.4 add lineto bxx 5.4 add byy 5.6 add lineto stroke } if
  0.7 setlinewidth } bind def
/TR 8 F (Verified in Ghostscript 10.07: 200 tokens, about 80 tok/s, about 3.5 MB of VM) /st1 exch def
/TB 8 F (Not yet run on a physical printer) /st2 exch def
/st2x RM 10 sub st2 sw sub def
LM 62 add BB 6.6 add true box6
/TR 8 F LM 73 add BB 7.3 add moveto st1 show
st2x 11 sub BB 6.6 add false box6
/TB 8 F st2x BB 7.3 add moveto st2 show

